## Part (a)

**Claim:** The only solutions are $P(x) = x + c$ and $P(x) = -x + c$ for any real constant $c$.

**Lemma.** Let $S = \{a_1, \dots, a_m\}$ with $m \ge 2$ and all $a_i$ distinct. If $f: S \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfies $|f(a_i) - f(a_j)| = |a_i - a_j|$ for all $i,j$, then there exists $c \in \mathbb{R}$ such that either $f(a_k) = a_k + c$ for all $k$, or $f(a_k) = -a_k + c$ for all $k$.

*Proof of Lemma.* Set $b_1 = f(a_1)$, $b_2 = f(a_2)$ (with $a_1 \ne a_2$). Since $|b_2 - b_1| = |a_2 - a_1|$, we have $b_2 - b_1 = \pm(a_2 - a_1)$.

**Case 1: $b_2 - b_1 = a_2 - a_1$.** Let $c = b_1 - a_1$. For any $k$, the condition $|f(a_k) - b_1| = |a_k - a_1|$ gives $f(a_k) = b_1 \pm (a_k - a_1)$, i.e., $f(a_k) = a_k + c$ or $f(a_k) = -a_k + (b_1 + a_1)$. The second option gives $f(a_k) - b_2 = -a_k + 2a_1 - a_2$, and requiring $|f(a_k) - b_2| = |a_k - a_2|$ forces $|-a_k + 2a_1 - a_2| = |a_k - a_2|$, which yields either $a_k = a_1$ or $a_1 = a_2$ — both contradictions for $k \ge 3$. So $f(a_k) = a_k + c$ for all $k \ge 3$, and this is verified for $k=1,2$ as well.

**Case 2: $b_2 - b_1 = -(a_2 - a_1)$.** Let $c = b_1 + a_1$. A symmetric argument shows $f(a_k) = -a_k + c$ for all $k$. $\square$

**Applying the Lemma to $P$:** Since $n \ge 1$ (positive integer) and $m > n$, we have $m \ge 2$, so the Lemma applies: $P$ agrees with either $x + c$ or $-x + c$ on all $m$ points $\{a_1, \dots, a_m\}$.

Both $x + c$ and $-x + c$ are polynomials of degree $1 \le n$. The difference $P(x) - (x+c)$ (or $P(x) - (-x+c)$) is a polynomial of degree at most $n$ that vanishes at all $m > n$ points. A nonzero polynomial of degree $\le n$ has at most $n$ roots, so this difference is identically zero.

Therefore $P(x) = x + c$ or $P(x) = -x + c$.

**Verification:** For $P(x) = x + c$: $|P(a_i) - P(a_j)| = |a_i - a_j|$. ✓  
For $P(x) = -x + c$: $|P(a_i) - P(a_j)| = |{-a_i + c} - ({-a_j + c})| = |a_j - a_i| = |a_i - a_j|$. ✓

