## Part (b)

**Claim:** Yes, such a polynomial $Q$ always exists.

**Construction:** Take $Q(x) = \varepsilon \, x^n$ for a suitable $\varepsilon > 0$.

This is a polynomial of degree exactly $n$ (since $\varepsilon \ne 0$). We need $|Q(a_i) - Q(a_j)| < |a_i - a_j|$ for all $i < j$, i.e.,

$$\varepsilon\, |a_i^n - a_j^n| < |a_i - a_j| \quad \text{for all } i < j.$$

**For pairs with $a_i^n = a_j^n$:** The left side is $0 < |a_i - a_j|$ (since $a_i \ne a_j$). The condition is automatically satisfied for any $\varepsilon > 0$.

**For pairs with $a_i^n \ne a_j^n$:** We need $\varepsilon < \dfrac{|a_i - a_j|}{|a_i^n - a_j^n|}$. The right-hand side is a positive real number for each such pair.

Since there are finitely many pairs $(i,j)$ with $i < j$, define:

$$\varepsilon_0 = \min\left\{ \frac{|a_i - a_j|}{|a_i^n - a_j^n|} : i < j,\; a_i^n \ne a_j^n \right\} > 0.$$

(If all pairs satisfy $a_i^n = a_j^n$, set $\varepsilon_0 = 1$; the condition is already satisfied for all pairs.)

Choose any $\varepsilon \in (0, \varepsilon_0)$. Then $Q(x) = \varepsilon x^n$ has degree exactly $n$ and satisfies $|Q(a_i) - Q(a_j)| < |a_i - a_j|$ for all $i < j$.

**Therefore, the answer to part (b) is yes.** $\blacksquare$

